Introduction

AWS CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service used to monitor AWS resources, applications, metrics, logs, and operational health.

As a DevOps Engineer, CloudWatch is very useful for monitoring production infrastructure and troubleshooting application issues.

AWS CloudWatch provides metrics, logs, alarms, dashboards, and other monitoring capabilities.

What is AWS CloudWatch?
 
Amazon CloudWatch helps us monitor AWS resources and applications in real time.

For example, we can monitor:  

EC2 CPU utilization
EC2 network traffic
Application logs
Lambda execution
RDS metrics
ALB metrics
EKS workloads
Application performance

CloudWatch can collect and store logs and metrics from AWS resources and applications.

Important CloudWatch Components
1. Metrics

Metrics are numerical data points that show the performance of a resource.

For example, EC2 provides metrics such as:

CPUUtilization
NetworkIn
NetworkOut
DiskReadOps
DiskWriteOps

Example:

EC2 CPUUtilization = 85%

If CPU continuously stays at 85% or higher, we can investigate whether the server needs more resources or whether an application is consuming excessive CPU.

2. Logs

CloudWatch Logs allows us to collect and store application and system logs.

Example:

Application Error
Database connection failed
HTTP 500

We can send application logs from EC2, Lambda, containers, and other services to CloudWatch Logs.

CloudWatch Logs organizes logs using:

Log Group
    |
    ├── Log Stream 1
    ├── Log Stream 2
    └── Log Stream 3
3. Alarms

CloudWatch Alarms monitor metrics and perform actions when a defined threshold is reached.

Example:

CPUUtilization > 80%
        |
        v
CloudWatch Alarm
        |
        v
SNS Notification

For example, if an EC2 instance CPU goes above 80%, we can configure an alarm to send a notification.

4. Dashboards

CloudWatch Dashboards provide a visual view of infrastructure and application metrics.

A DevOps Engineer can create a dashboard containing:

EC2 CPU
Memory
Network Traffic
ALB Requests
ALB 5XX Errors
RDS CPU
Application Logs

This makes it easier to monitor production systems from one place.

CloudWatch Agent

By default, EC2 provides several standard metrics such as CPU utilization.

If we need additional operating-system-level metrics such as:

Memory
Disk Usage
Swap Usage
Process Information

we can use the CloudWatch Agent.

The CloudWatch Agent can collect system-level metrics and logs from Linux and Windows servers.

Important Difference
CloudWatch Metrics vs Logs
Metrics	Logs
Numerical data	Text/event data
Used for monitoring performance	Used for troubleshooting
CPU utilization	Application errors
Network traffic	Application logs
Memory usage	System logs

Example:

Metric:
CPUUtilization = 95%

Log:
Application process consuming high CPU

We can use both together to troubleshoot production issues.

Practical Scenario
Scenario: EC2 CPU is 95%

Suppose an application running on EC2 suddenly becomes slow.

First, I would check CloudWatch metrics.

EC2
 |
 └── CPUUtilization = 95%

Then I would check:

CloudWatch Logs
       |
       v
Application logs
       |
       v
Find errors or unusual activity

I would then connect to the EC2 instance and check the running processes.

top

or

htop

I can also check memory:

free -m

Disk usage:

df -h

Finally, I would identify the root cause and take the appropriate action.

